---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="History"
  description="The story of the General Henry Champion House, Colonel Henry Champion, General Henry Champion, and the Champion family of Colchester, Connecticut."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="relative">
    <img
      src="/images/history-archival.jpg"
      alt="Archival photograph of the General Henry Champion House"
      class="h-[50vh] w-full object-cover"
    />
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-black/60 via-black/10 to-transparent"></div>
    <div class="absolute inset-0 flex items-end">
      <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-6 pb-12 text-white">
        <p class="eyebrow text-white/90">Colchester, Connecticut &middot; Built 1790</p>
        <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl md:text-5xl">A House Built for a General</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-xl text-lg text-white/90">
          Two Champions, a Revolutionary War, and 230 years of history on Westchester Road.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16">
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl">Built by a Father, for a Son</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        The house was built in 1790 by Colonel Henry Champion, a Revolutionary War officer, for his son,
        General Henry Champion &mdash; likely timed to the General's marriage to Abigail Tinker that same
        year. It was designed by William Spratt, the same house joiner who designed the Epaphroditus
        Champion House in East Haddam and the Deming House in Litchfield. General Champion lived here
        until his death in 1836.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl">Colonel Henry Champion &mdash; Feeding an Army</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        Colonel Henry Champion (1723&ndash;1797) served as Commissary General for the Eastern Department
        during the Revolutionary War, responsible for keeping Washington's army fed. His best-known act
        came in the brutal winter of 1777&ndash;78: working with his son Epaphroditus, he personally drove
        a herd of cattle 300 miles to reach the starving Continental Army at Valley Forge &mdash; a supply
        effort that involved direct coordination with both General Washington and Connecticut Governor
        Trumbull.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl">General Henry Champion &mdash; A Life at the House</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        General Henry Champion (1751&ndash;1836) was born and died in this part of Colchester, then known
        as Westchester. He answered the Lexington Alarm in 1775 and fought at Bunker Hill. In 1779, as
        acting major of the First Battalion, Light Brigade, he served under Washington's direct command at
        the capture of Stony Point &mdash; a moment he marked every year afterward, hosting a "Stony Point
        Day" celebration at this house each July 15 for the rest of his life.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        After the war, he remained active in public life: a deputy to the Connecticut General Assembly, an
        Assistant to the Governor, a judge on the state's Supreme Court of Errors, and a deacon of the
        Westchester church. He was also a major investor in the Connecticut Land Company &mdash; the towns
        of Champion, New York and Champion, Ohio are named for him, and his portrait at the Wadsworth
        Atheneum shows him with a map of the Western Reserve lands he helped settle.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl">An Architectural Record</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        The house's Federal-period details are well documented in its 1972 National Register nomination: a
        gambrel roof with a long rear slope nearly reaching a lean-to, twin brick chimneys, an
        Ionic-columned and triangular-pedimented entry portico, matching pedimented dormers, and
        twelve-over-twelve sash windows throughout the main house. A one-and-a-half-story ell extends to
        the south, later fitted with a conservatory. A historic stone wall with iron gates still marks the
        property's edge along the road.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl">Nationally Recognized</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        The Champion House holds two federal historic designations. It was added to the National and State
        Registers of Historic Places on October 10, 1972 (NR reference #72001323). It's also a stop on the
        Washington&ndash;Rochambeau Revolutionary Route, a National Park Service&ndash;designated National
        Historic Trail retracing the march of the Continental and French armies in 1781 and 1782.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 border-t border-[var(--color-stone)] pt-8 text-sm text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Sources / Further Reading</p>
      <p>
        Much of this history comes from the Colchester Historical Society's own research on the Champion
        family, along with the original 1972 National Register nomination. For more, visit the
        <a
          href="https://www.colchesterhistory.org/museum/exhibits/the-story-of-the-champion-family/"
          class="text-[var(--color-brick)] underline"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Colchester Historical Society</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
